From 85b1ed3975e0170ac9827c11e647d6d92e7d9372 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:15:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] daemon, app: send and receive files over RCS through IMSroha

A file to one number whose capabilities say chat and ft-http goes with
IMSroha's SendFile, each file as an RCS message of its own and any text
after them as an RCS chat message, with sent, delivered and displayed as
for text. The first file keeps the id the app shows the message under.
Each file is also copied into the conversation's attachments, the way a
sent MMS keeps its parts, so it looks like one. SendFile refusing the
first file sends the whole message as MMS, as before; refusing a later
one sends that file as MMS of its own, and so does a failed status
before sent (the upload failed). Several recipients, or no IMSroha, go
the MMS way unchanged.

A received file (FileReceived) is moved from IMSroha's download
directory, which it creates as the directory's owner, into the
conversation's attachments and stored like an MMS part, with transport
rcs and the usual notification. Its thumbnail is removed: the
conversation shows the file itself. Reading the chat sends the displayed
notification, as for text. A file that did not arrive
(FileReceiveFailed) becomes a short notice saying why, with no displayed
notification for it.

sendMessage now does its work in a member coroutine that takes copies,
not in a capturing lambda: the lambda is destroyed at the first
co_await and its captures with it, which a file send (several co_awaits
before the message is built) crashed on. The coroutines started from
IMSroha's signals take their arguments by value for the same reason.

The app adds a message it does not have yet when the daemon updates it
(the extra messages of an RCS send), and picks up a message's transport
when it changes.

